  <info><![CDATA[## WHAT IS IT?

A stylised agent-based model of lower-income renter households in a Melbourne-like region of 20 neighbourhoods on a ring, in three employment-accessibility tiers. It asks what allocation strategies can make a fixed supply of new affordable rental dwellings protect lower-income renters from involuntary displacement. It compares a no-new-supply baseline with three ways of allocating the same number of new dwellings: concentrated in the six high-accessibility neighbourhoods, proportional to current demand (stressed plus unhoused households), or dispersed evenly over all 20.

The model separates the two things a new affordable unit does for a household, a rent discount and a secure tenancy, so that a strategy's effect can be attributed to one or the other. It also includes an equation-based (mean-field) version of the same rates, to show what individual heterogeneity and matching add.

## HOW IT WORKS

Households are the only agents (1,000 by default). The middle row of patches holds the 20 neighbourhoods: six high tier (accessibility 1.0) in the middle, eight medium (0.6) either side, six low (0.3) at the ends, which touch across the wrap. The other rows only hold the household dots. Neighbourhoods form a ring, and n-shortcuts adds random edges between non-adjacent neighbourhoods, standing for transport corridors. Distance is the number of steps on that network.

Each tick is one quarter and a run is 40 quarters. Rents and incomes are shares of one reference quarterly income. In order, each quarter:

1. **Incomes.** Base incomes grow by income-growth. A pre-drawn shock (probability p-income-shock) cuts income by income-shock-size for shock-duration quarters.
2. **Tenancies end.** Market tenancies end with hazard termination-hazard-base x (1 + termination-hazard-coef x rent pressure). Affordable tenancies are exempt while secure-tenure? is on. With non-target-competition? on, dwellings held by higher-income renters are released at termination-hazard-base.
3. **Rents.** Pressure is (D - S) / S, where D counts occupied dwellings, higher-income renters' dwellings and their standing demand, and, with rent-feedback? on, last quarter's applicants. S is market stock plus affordable capacity. Rent moves by alpha x pressure, with pressure capped at plus or minus 0.5 and rent kept between 0.5 and 3 times its starting value.
4. **Stress.** A household is stressed when rent exceeds stress-threshold of its income. Unhoused households count as stressed.
5. **Search and matching.** Unhoused households search. Stressed market tenants search with probability search-prob. Any market tenant searches with probability bg-target-move-prob for reasons unrelated to stress. A searcher looks at its own neighbourhood and n-search-towns - 1 others, and with induced-demand? on also sees every affordable vacancy. Options costing more than max-rent-to-income of its income are dropped, and a stressed tenant only considers something cheaper than it pays now. It applies to one option, chosen by search-rule (by default a softmax on rent burden, accessibility, moving cost and last quarter's queue). With non-target-competition? on, higher-income renters first take a share non-target-frac of each market vacancy. Market vacancies then go by lottery, and affordable vacancies by priority-rule. A household unhoused for s-search-period quarters enters the displaced pool, loses income-penalty of its base income for good, and keeps searching.
6. **Supply.** New affordable units arrive over govt-homes-build-time quarters under the chosen strategy, with largest-remainder rounding. Demand-proportional allocation recounts demand at each tranche.

Affordable rent is affordable-rent-ratio x market rent under the "market-share" rule (market rent when rent-discount? is off), or income-rent-share x the tenant's income under the "income-share" rule.

With fixed-budget? on, govt-homes becomes a budget in medium-tier dwelling equivalents, and each tier's cost index (cost-high, cost-mid, cost-low) converts it into fewer dwellings where land is dearer. Strategies are then compared for the same spending rather than the same number of units.

## HOW TO USE IT

Set a seed, choose a strategy and priority rule, press setup, then go. A run takes about two seconds. Runs that share a seed share the same households, income shocks and termination draws whatever the strategy, so strategies can be compared as paired runs.

The interface opens on the calibrated set used for every result in the report: incomes uniform between 0.6 and 1.0, income-growth 0.01, bg-target-move-prob 0.15, termination-hazard-base 0.006, non-target-frac 0.30 / 0.15 / 0 by tier, max-rent-to-income 0.5, non-target-competition? on, alpha 0.03, priority-rule "lottery" for every strategy, with induced-demand?, rent-feedback?, secure-tenure? and rent-discount? all on. If you change settings, File > Revert returns to this set.

At the calibrated set the baseline reproduces all four calibration patterns (50 seeds): stress prevalence 0.87 in the first year and at quarter 40 (P1), displacement 0.28 at quarter 40, a forced share of moves of 0.31 (P2), a rent index of 1.55, 1.45 and 1.38 in the high, medium and low tiers (P3), and accessibility lost of 0.34 for displaced households against 0.04 for the rest (P4).

Switches for the mechanism experiments: induced-demand? and rent-feedback? remove the two ways new supply can undo itself. secure-tenure? and rent-discount? split a unit's protection into security and discount. search-rule offers three theories of household choice: "utility" (default), "cheapest" and "random" (the null theory). landlord-screening? matches applicants without a displacement record first, and displaced-leave? removes displaced households from the region. Both are off by default. mean-field? runs the equation-based version. Switch it on, then press setup.

The key output, displaced-share, counts entries into the displaced pool per household, so a household displaced twice counts twice. The by-tier and by-income reporters count households.

BehaviorSpace experiments in this file, by purpose. Checks: smoke-test, switches-check, budget-check. Calibration: pilot-v1 to pilot-v4 (the four pilot grids, with v1 and v2 re-run on this model with the later processes switched off), pilot-v_final (144 sets, 50 seeds), unswept-search-prob, unswept-hazard-coef, unswept-rents, ridge-check, screening-check, theory-test, theory-test-50 (the ranking under each search rule over 50 seeds). Stage 1: stage1-supply, stage1-window, stage1-priority, window-diagnostic. Stage 2: stage2-pooled, stage2-fixed-budget. Stage 3: stage3-counter-mechanisms, stage3-unit-decomposition, induced-demand-diagnostic, induced-demand-diagnostic-all (adds demand-proportional). Robustness: network-check, network-check-30, assumption-reversal, and sixteen one-parameter sensitivity runs (sensitivity-alpha, -hazard-coef, -weight-rent, -choice-noise, -n-search-towns, -s-search-period, -p-income-shock, -termination-hazard-base, -stress-threshold, -non-target-frac-high, -affordable-rent-ratio, -income-penalty, -n-households, -govt-homes, -income-growth, -cost-high).

## THINGS TO NOTICE

Each neighbourhood patch is coloured by tier (red high, orange medium, yellow low), darker where rent pressure is lower. Its label is rent as a percentage of its starting value. Household dots: green is a market tenant not in stress, red a market tenant in stress, blue a tenant in an affordable unit, white an unhoused household.

Watch the "aff. tenants stressed" monitor under the concentrated strategy. A 25 per cent discount on the region's highest rents still leaves about four in five of those tenants in stress.

## THINGS TO TRY

Same seed, rent-feedback? on then off: rents flatten, and under concentration displacement falls by about 0.04.

Concentrated strategy, induced-demand? on then off: displacement barely changes. With dispersed supply it falls by about 0.01.

secure-tenure? off: displacement rises by about 0.01 at the calibrated hazard, because tenancies rarely end at 0.6 per cent a quarter.

rent-discount? off: displacement barely changes, but stress lasts 0.7 to 1.5 quarters longer on average.

affordable-rent-rule set to "income-share": stress among affordable tenants disappears, because 25 per cent of income is below the 30 per cent stress threshold.

fixed-budget? on: concentration buys 64 dwellings with the money that buys 100 elsewhere, and its advantage reverses.

## EXTENDING THE MODEL

Households entering the region from outside, landlord agents, a real neighbourhood graph with travel times, and a global sensitivity analysis.

## CREDITS AND REFERENCES

COMP90083 Computational Modelling and Simulation, University of Melbourne, Semester 2, 2026. Ivan Ramos Sampe Immanuel and Fella Ulandari. Design precedents: Pangallo, Nadal and Vignes (2019) doi:10.1016/j.jebo.2019.01.010; Jackson, Forest and Sengupta (2008) doi:10.1111/j.1467-9671.2008.01109.x; Eckerd, Kim and Campbell (2019) doi:10.1080/10511482.2018.1512512; Hulse and Milligan (2014) doi:10.1080/02673037.2013.873116; Grimm et al. (2020) doi:10.18564/jasss.4259.]]></info>
